<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-v3w9n — tumbler grammar data remediation (Sam decision,
    2026-08-28, T1 scratch cb2f86a6 design-of-record).

    PROBLEM: owner prefixes ("1.N") and document tumblers share a namespace
    only when a document carries a 2-segment tumbler. Live census
    2026-08-28 (all 19,201 documents): exactly two exist, 1.1 and 1.2,
    registered 2026-05-22 under nonexistent owner "1" (no owner has a
    1-segment prefix), chunk_count 0, no collection, legacy 32-hex
    t3_chunk_id, one `cites` link 1.2 -> 1.1 — phantom registrations. The
    minting path (CatalogRepository#registerDocumentWithOutcome,
    `ownerPrefix + "." + claimed`) cannot produce them; the only routes
    that could are the legacy POST /v1/catalog/register raw upsert
    (CatalogHandler#handleRegister -> upsertDocument, honouring an
    explicit `tumbler`) and an explicit 1-segment `tumbler_prefix` on
    owner register (CatalogRepository#upsertOwner, honoured for
    ETL/import, no shape validation). No CHECK existed on either column
    before this changelog.

    DECISION: forbid both illegal shapes at the grammar, declaratively
    (RDR-154 discipline; precedent catalog-016's UNIQUE index). Owner
    prefix: exactly 2 dot-separated segments. Document tumbler: 3 or
    more dot-separated segments. Never widen (LOCKED INVARIANT).

    AMENDMENT 1 (orchestrator, 2026-08-28; T1 scratch 4cd73175): the
    owner's decision was a SEGMENT-COUNT grammar, not a numeric one — the
    original `[0-9]+` predicates were the orchestrator's own
    over-specification, not something Sam decided. Numeric-ness of
    segments is the Python client's `Tumbler.parse` concern (int-
    segmented) and stays enforced there, unchanged; the schema only
    counts segments (non-empty, dot-free). Evidence for the correction:
    the developer's Java-suite census found ~220 non-conforming literals
    across 10 test files under the numeric predicate, the overwhelming
    majority of them mnemonic-prefixed shapes (e.g. "bt.1.5",
    "cov2.1.1") that satisfy the count grammar and only ever failed the
    numeric one.

    AMENDMENT 2 (orchestrator, 2026-08-28; T1 scratch 6ba1a27d; owner
    decision after the full-suite measurement — 1959 tests, 331 broken
    across 46+ classes, T1 58945409): the schema CHECK constraints are
    DEFERRED. The engine test corpus is shaped 1-segment-owner /
    2-segment-document throughout — raw-SQL fixtures and shared test
    scaffolding included, not just the ~13 files a syntactic-context
    census could find — so landing the CHECKs now would require a
    fixture-normalization effort far larger than this bead, discovered
    only via the actual failing-suite count, not by inspection. The
    grammar is enforced at the engine's HTTP API boundary INSTEAD (see
    CatalogHandler's `TumblerGrammar` validator, nexus-v3w9n) — every
    EXTERNAL producer enters through the HTTP handlers, and internal
    minting (`ownerPrefix + "." + seq`) already conforms, so the boundary
    is where an illegal shape can actually be introduced today. This
    file therefore keeps ONLY the data remediation (034-0 below); the
    two CHECK constraints are deferred to **nexus-ia69x** (the fixture-
    normalization bead), which re-adds them verbatim once the engine
    test suite conforms:

        catalog_owners_prefix_grammar_ck:
            ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_owners
                ADD CONSTRAINT catalog_owners_prefix_grammar_ck
                CHECK (tumbler_prefix ~ '^[^.\s]+\.[^.\s]+$')
                NOT VALID;
            then: ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_owners
                      VALIDATE CONSTRAINT catalog_owners_prefix_grammar_ck;

        catalog_documents_tumbler_grammar_ck:
            ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_documents
                ADD CONSTRAINT catalog_documents_tumbler_grammar_ck
                CHECK (deleted_at IS NOT NULL OR tumbler ~ '^[^.\s]+(\.[^.\s]+){2,}$')
                NOT VALID;
            then: ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_documents
                      VALIDATE CONSTRAINT catalog_documents_tumbler_grammar_ck;

        Both NOT VALID -> VALIDATE, matching catalog-029's three-step
        shape; the document CHECK's `deleted_at IS NOT NULL OR ...` exempts
        tombstoned rows because VALIDATE CONSTRAINT scans every row
        including tombstoned ones, and 034-0 below tombstones (never
        deletes) any pre-existing violator, leaving its original 2-segment
        shape in place as forensic history — a bare `tumbler ~ regex`
        CHECK would fail VALIDATE on the very rows this changelog fixed.
        catalog_owners_prefix_grammar_ck needs no remediation step (the
        2026-08-28 census found zero violating owners in production); the
        document CHECK's VALIDATE must run strictly after 034-0.

        FIX ROUND 1 (code-review-expert Important finding): both predicates
        above carry `\s` in the negated character class (`[^.\s]+`, not
        `[^.]+`) — a bare `[^.]+` accepts a whitespace-only segment (e.g.
        `"1. "` or `"1.\t"`), which the HTTP boundary validator
        (`TumblerGrammar`, CatalogHandler's live enforcement point today)
        was fixed to reject in the same round. nexus-ia69x must inherit
        the closed gap, not the original text.

    ONE CHANGESET remains in this file:

      034-0 (DATA): tombstone the two live ghost rows (and any other live
        catalog_documents row with fewer than three NON-EMPTY dot-separated
        segments — fix round 1, substantive-critic Significant finding: the
        predicate also catches a nominally-3+-segment tumbler carrying an
        empty segment, e.g. "1.2." or "1..3"; the comment previously said
        "sub-3-segment", which undersold what the shipped SQL actually
        matches) — tombstone, never physically delete (RDR-156 D6; the catalog-003
        trash flow). RLS toggle-wrapped (nexus-1wjmq shape, catalog-016-0
        / catalog-029-1 precedent): catalog_documents carries FORCE ROW
        LEVEL SECURITY (catalog-001-baseline.xml) and Liquibase's
        nexus_admin owner is NOT BYPASSRLS, so an untoggled UPDATE would
        silently affect ZERO rows in production. On every FRESH install /
        test container this is a genuine no-op: no non-conforming row can
        exist before this changeset runs — it only does real work against
        the one production database carrying 1.1/1.2. This step is
        independent of the deferred CHECKs (tombstoning phantom rows is
        correct regardless of whether a CHECK enforces the shape) and
        ships now rather than waiting on nexus-ia69x.
        INTENTIONALLY IRREVERSIBLE (catalog-016-0 precedent): empty
        <rollback/> declares that explicitly rather than leaving it
        implied — there is no schema change left in this file to revert
        around it.

    Placed after telemetry-007-nx-answer-steps.xml (current tail) and
    before the grants-nexus-svc.xml runAlways block, matching the
    established late-append convention (D0.1).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <!-- ── Changeset 034-0: tombstone fewer-than-3-non-empty-segment documents (data) ────────── -->
    <changeSet id="catalog-034-0" author="nexus-v3w9n">
        <comment>
            Tombstone every LIVE catalog_documents row whose tumbler has fewer
            than three NON-EMPTY dot-separated segments (the 2026-08-28 census
            found exactly two: 1.1 and 1.2, phantom registrations under a
            nonexistent 1-segment owner). Segment-count grammar (AMENDMENT 1)
            — segments need not be numeric. The predicate below also catches
            a nominally-3+-segment tumbler with an empty segment (e.g.
            "1.2." or "1..3") — fix round 1: the comment now says exactly
            what the predicate matches, not "sub-3-segment"; no live row of
            that shape exists today (verified against the 19,201-doc
            2026-08-28 census). RLS toggle-wrapped: catalog_documents is
            FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY and the Liquibase role is not
            BYPASSRLS.
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_documents NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_tombstoned_count BIGINT;
BEGIN
    UPDATE nexus.catalog_documents
       SET deleted_at = NOW()
     WHERE deleted_at IS NULL
       AND tumbler !~ '^[^.]+(\.[^.]+){2,}$';
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_tombstoned_count = ROW_COUNT;
    RAISE NOTICE 'nexus-v3w9n catalog-034 tumbler-grammar remediation: tombstoned % live catalog_documents row(s) with fewer than 3 non-empty tumbler segments', v_tombstoned_count;
END $$;

ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_documents FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
        </sql>
        <!--
            INTENTIONALLY IRREVERSIBLE (catalog-016-0 precedent): the rows
            tombstoned here are phantom registrations no live write path can
            reproduce; a schema rollback must not resurrect them, and this
            changeset records nothing about which rows it touched, so there is
            no honest inverse.
        -->
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
